In the microscopic theory of nuclear many-body dynamics, large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) refers to slow, large-amplitude time evolution of a self-bound fermionic many-body system that can be described, in even-even nuclei, by a small number of collective coordinates parametrizing a manifold of generalized Slater determinants (e.g., via the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method, the constrained Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov plus adiabatic time-dependent HFB approach, or the generator-coordinate method). For systems built on a quasiparticle vacuum these methods provide non-perturbative collective Hamiltonians coupling shape and pairing degrees of freedom.

For odd-mass nuclei, and more generally for fermionic many-body states containing one or more unpaired quasiparticles on top of a deforming quasiparticle vacuum, the existing non-perturbative LACM frameworks do not directly apply, because the presence of blocked quasiparticle states breaks the simple structure of the collective manifold and couples single-particle motion of the odd quasiparticle(s) to the collective shape/pairing dynamics in an essential way. The standard alternative, particle–vibration coupling, is intrinsically a small-amplitude (perturbative) expansion around a static mean field.

Problem. Construct a microscopic, fully non-perturbative theoretical framework that describes large-amplitude collective motion in odd-A nuclei, and more generally in self-consistent mean-field systems with several blocked quasiparticles, in such a way that:
(i) it does not rely on a small-amplitude or perturbative particle–vibration-coupling expansion;
(ii) it treats the coupling between the intrinsic motion of the unpaired quasiparticle(s) and the collective (shape and pairing) degrees of freedom on the same footing, yielding a unified set of collective coordinates, momenta, and a collective Hamiltonian;
(iii) it reduces, in the limit of an even-even quasiparticle vacuum, to an established non-perturbative LACM theory (such as the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method or adiabatic TDHFB);
(iv) it provides computable equations (e.g., self-consistent equations determining the collective submanifold and inertial functions) suitable for application to realistic effective interactions or energy density functionals.

